      "content": "You're right to feel that way. The way interest is compounded in loans can be a bit counterintuitive. However, it's not necessarily \"sneaky\" – it's just a fundamental aspect of how loans work.\n\nThat being said, there are a few strategies you can use to try to reduce the amount of interest you pay over the life of the loan. One approach is to make extra payments, which can help to reduce the principal balance and, in turn, reduce the amount of interest you owe.\n\nFor example, if you make a lump sum payment of $1,000, that would reduce the principal balance to $9,000. Since the interest is calculated based on the new principal balance, the interest for the next month would be lower, and the snowball effect would be slowed down.\n\nAnother strategy is to consider refinancing your loan to a loan with a lower interest rate. This can help to reduce the amount of interest you pay over the life of the loan, but it may also involve paying fees or adjusting your payment schedule.\n\nIt's worth noting that some loans, such as income-driven repayment plans, can help to reduce the amount of interest you pay by capping your monthly payments at a percentage of your income",
